# Call-to-receiver mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Replaces the last call of a chain with its receiver, with the arguments
# and the block of that call: `user.full_name` becomes `user`, and
# `ids.uniq.sort` becomes `ids.uniq`. A chain begins at a receiver that is
# not a dotted call, as in {ChainLink}. A call in an argument or a block
# starts a chain of its own.
#
# The mutant survives when no test checks what the last call does: a strip,
# a uniq, a compact or a lookup the tests never see.
#
# The operator does not overlap with {ChainLink}. `chain_link` drops a link
# in the middle of a chain, and this operator drops the last call. A call
# that is the receiver of a dotted call, or of a call write or a call
# target, is a link, so it gets no mutant here. The two operators give the
# same source only when the last call repeats the link before it, as in
# `node.name.name`. When both edits start on the same line, the job plan
# runs one copy, the `chain_link` one (#275).
#
# Calls in {SKIPPED} are never replaced; each one counts as declined. The
# list is shorter than {ChainLink::SKIPPED}: on the last call, a conversion
# such as `to_s` or `to_i` decides the type of the value that the
# expression returns, so `m[1].to_i` becomes `m[1]`. `Foo.new` becomes the
# class, and almost any use of the class raises.
#
# A call is not replaced when its name is an operator (`a.+(1)`), or a
# setter (`a.b = 1`). A call with no dot (`a + b`, `a[0]`) is not replaced.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:34
class Mutineer::Mutators::CallToReceiver < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Resets the per-subject record of calls that are links.
  #
  # @param subject [Mutineer::Subject] subject whose body is visited.
  # @param source [String] full source text for byte-based slicing.
  # @return [Array<Mutineer::Mutation>] collected mutations.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:54
  def mutations_for(subject, source); end

  # Visits `a.b.c &&= 1`. Its receiver is a link.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallAndWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:92
  def visit_call_and_write_node(node); end

  # Visits a call. A dotted call that is not a link is the last call of its
  # chain.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:64
  def visit_call_node(node); end

  # Visits `a.b.c += 1`. Its receiver is a link.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:74
  def visit_call_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `a.b.c ||= 1`. Its receiver is a link.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallOrWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:83
  def visit_call_or_write_node(node); end

  # Visits a call used as an assignment target, as in `a.b.c, d = 1, 2`.
  # Its receiver is a link.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallTargetNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:102
  def visit_call_target_node(node); end

  private

  # Returns whether a node is a call through `.`, `&.` or `::`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node, nil] node to inspect.
  # @return [Boolean] true for a call node with a call operator.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:143
  def dotted?(node); end

  # Records the receiver of a node as a link when the node is a dotted call
  # or a call write or target. The receiver of a call with no dot is not a
  # link. It is the last call of a chain of its own.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node] node that responds to `receiver`.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:115
  def mark(node); end

  # Emits the replacement of a call by its receiver, unless the name is an
  # operator, a setter, or in {SKIPPED}.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] the dotted call to replace.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:124
  def replace(node); end
end

# Method names whose last call is never replaced. A name is here when
# its mutant is most often equivalent, or when almost any test that runs
# the line kills it. `freeze` and `itself` return the receiver. `dup`
# and `clone` return a copy, so the mutant shows when code later changes
# the copy or the original, or compares the result with the original by
# identity. Dropping `to_set` most often leaves an Array, which answers
# `include?` and `empty?` as a Set does (it keeps duplicates, so `size`
# and `each` can differ). `new` leaves the class in place of an
# instance, which almost any test that runs the line kills.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:47
Mutineer::Mutators::CallToReceiver::SKIPPED = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Array)

# Matches the name of a call written as a word, not as an operator.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/call_to_receiver.rb:36
Mutineer::Mutators::CallToReceiver::WORD_NAME = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Regexp)

# Chain-link mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Drops one dotted call from a chain of calls, with its arguments and block:
# `user.account.owner.name` becomes `user.owner.name` and `user.account.name`.
# The chain's receiver and its final call stay, so a chain of n dotted calls
# gives at most n - 1 mutations. A chain begins at a receiver that is not a
# dotted call: a local, a constant, `self`, `list[i]`, `(a + b)`. A call in
# an argument or a block starts a chain of its own.
#
# The mutant survives when no test tells the chain apart from the same chain
# without that step: a scope, a filter or a lookup the tests never see.
#
# Links in {SKIPPED} are never dropped; each one counts as declined. On a
# value that already has the target type or needs no copy, a conversion
# (`name.to_s.strip`) or a copy (`list.dup.sort`) is a no-op, so dropping
# it most often makes an equivalent mutant. Dropping `new` sends the next call to the class, which
# raises (killed by any test that runs the line) or reaches a class method
# that builds the instance itself (equivalent); neither says anything about
# the tests.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:26
class Mutineer::Mutators::ChainLink < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Resets the per-subject record of calls already placed in a chain.
  #
  # @param subject [Mutineer::Subject] subject whose body is visited.
  # @param source [String] full source text for byte-based slicing.
  # @return [Array<Mutineer::Mutation>] collected mutations.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:40
  def mutations_for(subject, source); end

  # Visits `a.b.c &&= 1`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallAndWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:77
  def visit_call_and_write_node(node); end

  # Visits a call. The outermost dotted call of a chain is its final call;
  # the dotted calls below it in the receiver are its links.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:50
  def visit_call_node(node); end

  # Visits `a.b.c += 1`, whose final call Prism parses as its own node.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:59
  def visit_call_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `a.b.c ||= 1`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallOrWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:68
  def visit_call_or_write_node(node); end

  # Visits a call used as an assignment target, as in `a.b.c, d = 1, 2`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallTargetNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:86
  def visit_call_target_node(node); end

  private

  # Walks a final call's receiver chain, recording each link so it is not
  # taken for the final call of a chain of its own, and dropping each link
  # not in {SKIPPED}.
  #
  # @param top [Prism::Node] the chain's final call; responds to `receiver`.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:99
  def chain(top); end

  # Returns whether a node is a call through `.`, `&.` or `::`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node, nil] node to inspect.
  # @return [Boolean] true for a call node with a call operator.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:127
  def dotted?(node); end

  # Emits the removal of one link: from the end of its receiver to the end
  # of its arguments and block, so a chain split across lines keeps the
  # layout of the lines that remain.
  #
  # @param link [Prism::CallNode] the dotted call to drop.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:114
  def drop(link); end
end

# Method names whose link is never dropped: core conversions and copies,
# plus `new`. {CallToReceiver} has its own shorter list for the last call.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/chain_link.rb:29
Mutineer::Mutators::ChainLink::SKIPPED = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Array)

# Collection / enumerable method-name mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Swaps the method-name token of a call to its semantic opposite, one
# mutation per occurrence, exactly like the arithmetic/comparison operators.
#
# `each` and `map` are the exception. `each` returns its receiver and
# `map` returns a new array. When that value is discarded, no test can
# tell the mutant from the original, so the swap is declined. The other
# swaps stay: `all?` and `any?` stop on different elements, so a block
# can show the change even when the boolean is discarded.
# See docs/equivalent-skips.md.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:18
class Mutineer::Mutators::CollectionMethod < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Records parents, then walks the body. The result check needs the
  # parent of each call, and Prism nodes do not store one.
  #
  # @param subject [Mutineer::Subject] subject whose body is visited.
  # @param source [String] full source text for byte-based slicing.
  # @return [Array<Mutineer::Mutation>] collected mutations.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:68
  def mutations_for(subject, source); end

  # Visits call nodes and emits collection-method mutations.
  # An {UNUSED_RESULT_NAMES} call whose value is discarded is declined.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:79
  def visit_call_node(node); end

  private

  # Stores the parent of every node under `node`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node, nil] subtree to index.
  # @param parent [Prism::Node, nil] parent of `node`, or nil at the root.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:104
  def index_parents(node, parent); end

  # Returns whether `node` is inside a `defined?` operand.
  #
  # `defined?` does not run that operand, so an `each` or `map` there
  # does not call `to_s`, run a command, or compile a regexp.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node] node to inspect.
  # @return [Boolean] true when an ancestor is a DefinedNode.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:140
  def inside_defined?(node); end

  # Returns whether a `lazy` call sits in this call's receiver chain.
  #
  # `xs.lazy.each` and `xs.lazy.map { }.each` both count. A lazy
  # enumerator stored in a variable does not: the tree no longer shows
  # the `lazy` call.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] `each` or `map` call to inspect.
  # @return [Boolean] true when the chain calls `lazy`.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:156
  def lazy_chain?(node); end

  # Returns whether any syntax reads this node's value.
  #
  # The method body, a block body, and a lambda body count as read: a
  # caller may use the method's return value, and the method that takes
  # a block may use the block's result. The operator does not guess
  # which methods do. A condition, an assignment, an argument, and a
  # receiver count as read even when the outer expression is discarded.
  # A `while` or `until` body does not, and neither does `ensure` or
  # `defined?`. A `for` body does not either: `for` returns the
  # collection, not the body. A hash key is read on insertion. The value
  # of a hash pair follows the hash. A later `rescue` clause follows the
  # `begin`, the same way the first clause does.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node] node whose value may flow outward.
  # @return [Boolean] true when the value is observable.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:181
  def result_used?(node); end

  # Returns whether this call is an `each` or `map` whose value is discarded.
  #
  # Interpolation is not discarded: a string or symbol calls `to_s`, a
  # backtick command runs, and a regexp is compiled. A hash key is not
  # discarded either: insertion calls `hash` and `eql?`. Those nodes are
  # absent from {VALUE_FLOWS}, so {#result_used?} keeps them. A receiver
  # chain that calls `lazy` is kept too. `Enumerator::Lazy#each` runs the
  # block and `Lazy#map` does not, and the syntax tree can see the `lazy`
  # call. A lazy enumerator stored in a variable has no `lazy` call left.
  # `defined?` does not evaluate its operand, so a call inside it is
  # discarded even when the chain calls `lazy` or the call sits in an
  # interpolation.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [Boolean] true when the swap would be equivalent.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:126
  def unused_result?(node); end
end

# Method-name swaps. All targets are core-Ruby Enumerable/Array methods so
# the mutant exercises real behaviour rather than always raising.
#
# include? -> exclude? is left out: exclude? is not
# core Ruby, so that mutant would always NoMethodError (a weak mutant).
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:24
Mutineer::Mutators::CollectionMethod::REPLACEMENTS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Hash)

# Names whose swap changes the returned object and not the walk.
# A discarded call is declined. The check is the syntax tree only:
# it does not know the receiver's class.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:35
Mutineer::Mutators::CollectionMethod::UNUSED_RESULT_NAMES = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Array)

# Parents that pass a child's value through. The child is observable
# exactly when that parent is. An assignment, an argument, a receiver,
# or an explicit `return` / `break` / `next` is not in this list: those
# parents use the value even when their own result is discarded.
# Interpolation is not in this list. A string or symbol calls `to_s`
# on the value, a backtick command runs, and a regexp is compiled,
# even when the expression's own result is thrown away. A hash
# association is not in this list either: its key is hashed on
# insertion, and its value is handled in {#result_used?}.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/collection_method.rb:46
Mutineer::Mutators::CollectionMethod::VALUE_FLOWS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Array)

# Comparison and boundary mutator.
#
# Rewrites comparison operators one occurrence at a time.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/comparison.rb:10
class Mutineer::Mutators::Comparison < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits call nodes and emits comparison mutations.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/comparison.rb:20
  def visit_call_node(node); end
end

# Token replacements for comparison operators.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/comparison.rb:12
Mutineer::Mutators::Comparison::REPLACEMENTS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Hash)

# Condition-forcing mutator: the condition becomes `false` (Tier-2).
#
# An `if` branch never runs; an `unless` branch always runs. The mutant
# survives when no test needs the branch the condition guards (for an
# `if`) or skips it (for an `unless`).
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:130
class Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionFalse < ::Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionForcing; end

# The operator name mutations carry.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:134
Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionFalse::OPERATOR = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Symbol)

# The literal the condition is replaced with.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:132
Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionFalse::VALUE = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), FalseClass)

# Shared base of the condition-forcing mutators (Tier-2).
#
# Replaces the condition of an `if`, `elsif`, `unless`, ternary, modifier
# guard (`x if y`) or `case`/`in` guard with a literal, so its branch always
# runs or never runs. {ConditionTrue} forces `true` and {ConditionFalse}
# forces `false`; one mutation per condition each.
#
# A condition that is already `true`, `false` or `nil`, even in
# parentheses, is left alone: forcing it changes nothing or repeats the
# `boolean_literal` flip. This holds even when `boolean_literal` does not run.
# A condition that holds a heredoc is left alone too: the heredoc body lies
# outside the condition, so it would stay behind as code. A condition that
# assigns a variable keeps its code and only its value is forced.
#
# The never-runs side of an else-less conditional can be the same program
# as the `nil` that `statement_removal` or `return_nil` puts in place of the
# whole conditional. It is still made: dropping it would make the mutants
# of one operator depend on which other operators run, and on what the user
# suppressed for them. The two then get the same verdict. Their text
# differs, so the job plan's drop of a repeated edit (#275) keeps both.
#
# `condition_negation` wraps the condition in `!( ... )`, which neither
# forced value is.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:30
class Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionForcing < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits if nodes: `if`, `elsif`, a ternary, a modifier `if` and an
  # `in ... if` guard.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IfNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:39
  def visit_if_node(node); end

  # Visits unless nodes: `unless`, a modifier `unless` and an
  # `in ... unless` guard.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::UnlessNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:49
  def visit_unless_node(node); end

  private

  # Emits the forced condition unless the condition is a literal or holds a
  # heredoc, whose body would stay behind as code. The heredoc skip counts
  # as declined; the literal skip does not.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IfNode, Prism::UnlessNode] the conditional.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:62
  def force(node); end

  # Returns the forced value for the condition, always in parentheses, so it
  # cannot fuse with a keyword or a `?` next to it: `x if@a` becomes
  # `x if(true)` and `@a?1:2` becomes `(true)?1:2`. A condition that assigns
  # a variable keeps its code, so later reads still see the variable:
  # `(m = x; true)`.
  #
  # @param predicate [Prism::Node] the condition.
  # @return [String] the replacement source.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:84
  def replacement(predicate); end

  # Returns the node inside parentheses that hold exactly one expression,
  # so `(true)` and `((true))` read as `true`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node] the condition.
  # @return [Prism::Node] the innermost wrapped node, or `node` itself.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:106
  def unwrap(node); end

  # Returns whether the node writes a variable or constant anywhere inside
  # it: a local, instance, class or global variable, or a constant, in a
  # plain, compound, multiple or pattern write, or a named regex capture.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node] the node to inspect.
  # @return [Boolean] true when a write or target node is inside.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:95
  def writes?(node); end
end

# Condition nodes that `boolean_literal` already mutates.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:32
Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionForcing::LITERALS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Array)

# Condition-negation mutator.
#
# Wraps if/unless predicates in `!( ... )` textually.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_negation.rb:10
class Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionNegation < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits if nodes.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IfNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_negation.rb:15
  def visit_if_node(node); end

  # Visits unless nodes.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::UnlessNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_negation.rb:24
  def visit_unless_node(node); end

  private

  # Wraps a predicate in negation.
  #
  # @param predicate [Prism::Node, nil] predicate node.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_negation.rb:35
  def wrap(predicate); end
end

# Condition-forcing mutator: the condition becomes `true` (Tier-2).
#
# An `if` branch always runs; an `unless` branch never runs. The mutant
# survives when no test runs the code with the condition false (for an
# `if`) or true (for an `unless`) and checks what changes.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:118
class Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionTrue < ::Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionForcing; end

# The operator name mutations carry.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:122
Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionTrue::OPERATOR = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Symbol)

# The literal the condition is replaced with.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/condition_forcing.rb:120
Mutineer::Mutators::ConditionTrue::VALUE = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), TrueClass)

# Literal mutation mutator.
#
# Mutates integers and strings with the Tier-2 literal rules.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/literal_mutation.rb:10
class Mutineer::Mutators::LiteralMutation < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits integer literals.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IntegerNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/literal_mutation.rb:15
  def visit_integer_node(node); end

  # Visits an interpolated string. In a `"..."` string, `""` over a text
  # part closes and reopens the string, so it empties the part. Any other
  # text part is handled as a string with no quotes of its own.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::InterpolatedStringNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/literal_mutation.rb:29
  def visit_interpolated_string_node(node); end

  # Visits string literals.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::StringNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/literal_mutation.rb:42
  def visit_string_node(node); end

  private

  # Emits a literal mutation.
  #
  # @param loc [Prism::Location] source location.
  # @param replacement [String] replacement literal.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/literal_mutation.rb:71
  def emit(loc, replacement); end

  # Empties a non-empty string. A string with no quotes of its own (a
  # heredoc line, a `%(...)` or `%Q(...)` part, a `%w[]` element, a regex,
  # backtick or interpolated-symbol part) cannot be emptied with `""`: the edit inserts text
  # and can break the parse (#469). Such a string is declined.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::StringNode] string to inspect.
  # @param quoted [Object] truthy when `""` empties the string.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/literal_mutation.rb:57
  def mutate_string(node, quoted:); end
end

# Negation-removal mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Removes the `!` or `not` of a negation, one mutation per occurrence:
# `!x` becomes `x` and `not x` becomes ` x`. The mutant survives when no
# test depends on the negated value.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/negation_removal.rb:12
class Mutineer::Mutators::NegationRemoval < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits call nodes and emits negation-removal mutations.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/negation_removal.rb:17
  def visit_call_node(node); end

  private

  # Emits a mutation when the node is a prefix `!` or `not`.
  #
  # The explicit form `x.!` is skipped: without its message, `x.` does
  # not parse.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/negation_removal.rb:31
  def emit(node); end
end

# Operand-removal mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Replaces a boolean expression with one of its operands, two mutations
# per `&&`, `||`, `and` or `or`: `a && b` becomes `(a)` and `(b)`. The
# parentheses keep the precedence of `and` and `or`. A surviving mutant
# shows that no test needs the operand that was removed.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operand_removal.rb:13
class Mutineer::Mutators::OperandRemoval < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits `and` nodes.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::AndNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operand_removal.rb:22
  def visit_and_node(node); end

  # Visits `or` nodes.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::OrNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operand_removal.rb:31
  def visit_or_node(node); end

  private

  # Emits one mutation that keeps the left operand, then one that keeps
  # the right operand. The order is fixed, because the two mutants share
  # a token and their occurrence ordinals tell them apart. A removed
  # operand that holds a heredoc counts as declined; a kept jump does not.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::AndNode, Prism::OrNode] connector node.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operand_removal.rb:45
  def emit(node); end
end

# Node types for jumps. A jump in a value context does not parse, so
# the operator never keeps a jump alone.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operand_removal.rb:16
Mutineer::Mutators::OperandRemoval::JUMPS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Array)

# Operator-assignment mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Swaps the operator of a compound assignment, one mutation per
# occurrence: `t += x` -> `t -= x`. Prism parses `t += x` as its own
# node, not as a CallNode, so the arithmetic mutator never sees it.
#
# The mapping is the same as for arithmetic. Other compound operators
# (`||=`, `&&=`, `|=`, `<<=` and the rest) are not changed.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:15
class Mutineer::Mutators::OperatorAssignment < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits `a.b += x` and `a&.b += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:79
  def visit_call_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `@@t += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::ClassVariableOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:43
  def visit_class_variable_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `T += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::ConstantOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:61
  def visit_constant_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `A::T += x` and `::T += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::ConstantPathOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:70
  def visit_constant_path_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `$t += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::GlobalVariableOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:52
  def visit_global_variable_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `a[i] += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IndexOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:88
  def visit_index_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `@t += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::InstanceVariableOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:34
  def visit_instance_variable_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `t += x`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::LocalVariableOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:25
  def visit_local_variable_operator_write_node(node); end

  private

  # Emits a mutation when the node's operator has a replacement.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node] a node with `binary_operator` and `binary_operator_loc`.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:99
  def emit(node); end
end

# Token replacements for compound arithmetic operators.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/operator_assignment.rb:17
Mutineer::Mutators::OperatorAssignment::REPLACEMENTS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Hash)

# Range mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Swaps `..` with `...` and `...` with `..`, one mutation per range. The
# `..` -> `...` mutant survives when no test checks the last element.
# Flip-flops are FlipFlopNode, not RangeNode, so this mutator skips them.
#
# An endless range (`1..`, `1..nil`) is skipped only when it is a direct
# index argument of a call in {SLICE_CALLS} or of an index write
# (`"ab"[1..] += x`) and the receiver is an array or string literal.
# There `(1..)` and `(1...)` select the same elements, so the mutant is
# equivalent, and each skipped range counts as declined. A variable, a
# call, or a constant is swapped. The syntax does not record that
# receiver's class, and a Hash, a Proc, or a custom `[]` can tell the two
# ranges apart. Every other endless range is swapped, because the two
# ranges differ on `==`, `hash`, `exclude_end?` and `to_s`, so a test can
# kill it. This includes the value of `h[:k] = 1..` and a call on a class,
# such as `Array[1..]`, which builds a value and does not slice.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:24
class Mutineer::Mutators::RangeLiteral < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Resets the per-subject record of endless ranges that are slice arguments.
  #
  # @param subject [Mutineer::Subject] subject whose body is visited.
  # @param source [String] full source text for byte-based slicing.
  # @return [Array<Mutineer::Mutation>] collected mutations.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:39
  def mutations_for(subject, source); end

  # Visits a call. Records the range arguments of a call in {SLICE_CALLS}
  # when the receiver is an array or string literal.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::CallNode] call node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:49
  def visit_call_node(node); end

  # Visits `a[1..] &&= x`. Records its index arguments when the receiver is
  # an array or string literal.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IndexAndWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:79
  def visit_index_and_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `a[1..] += x`. Records its index arguments when the receiver is
  # an array or string literal.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IndexOperatorWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:59
  def visit_index_operator_write_node(node); end

  # Visits `a[1..] ||= x`. Records its index arguments when the receiver is
  # an array or string literal.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IndexOrWriteNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:69
  def visit_index_or_write_node(node); end

  # Visits an index used as an assignment target, as in `a[1..], b = x, y`.
  # Records its index arguments when the receiver is an array or string literal.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::IndexTargetNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:89
  def visit_index_target_node(node); end

  # Visits range nodes and emits range mutations.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::RangeNode] range node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:98
  def visit_range_node(node); end

  private

  # Whether every value a `begin` can return is an array or string literal.
  # The value is the else, or the body when there is no else. Each rescue
  # can replace it. `ensure` runs but does not supply the value.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::BeginNode] begin expression used as a receiver.
  # @return [Boolean]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:155
  def begin_is_sequence?(node); end

  # Emits the mutation that swaps the range operator.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::RangeNode] range node to mutate.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:193
  def emit(node); end

  # Whether the range has no end: `1..` or `1..nil`.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::RangeNode] range node to inspect.
  # @return [Boolean]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:185
  def endless?(node); end

  # Records each range that is a direct argument of the node, when the
  # receiver is an array or string literal. For a `[]=` call the last
  # argument is the assigned value, not an index, so it is left out.
  # A variable, call, or constant is not recorded: its class is not fixed
  # by the syntax, so the range is swapped.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node] call or index node that responds to `arguments`.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:117
  def record_slice_args(node); end

  # Whether the receiver's syntax is an Array or a String. A quoted string
  # or an array literal is. Parentheses are unwrapped to the value they
  # return, so `("ab")[1..]` is still a string. A `begin` counts only when
  # every branch that can supply its value is one of those literals: a
  # rescue or an else can return a Hash instead. Backticks are a call to
  # `` ` ``, which can be overridden, so they are not literals. A variable,
  # call, or constant is not either: it can hold a Hash, a Proc, or a
  # custom object, and an earlier assignment is not followed.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node, nil] receiver of a slice call or index write.
  # @return [Boolean]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:136
  def sequence_receiver?(node); end

  # The value of a statement list, which is its last statement. Any other
  # node is itself.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::Node, nil] statements, or a single expression.
  # @return [Prism::Node, nil]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:177
  def statement_value(node); end
end

# Calls whose direct endless-range index arguments are not mutated when
# the receiver is an array or string literal. Those calls select the
# same elements for `(1..)` and `(1...)`. The assigned value of `[]=`
# is not an index, so it is mutated.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:32
Mutineer::Mutators::RangeLiteral::SLICE_CALLS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Array)

# Maps each range operator to its opposite.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/range_literal.rb:26
Mutineer::Mutators::RangeLiteral::SWAPS = T.let(T.unsafe(nil), Hash)

# Regular-expression literal mutator (Tier-2).
#
# Conservative, clearly-safe textual swaps inside a plain regex literal:
# drop a leading +^+ anchor, drop a trailing +$+ anchor, and swap the +
# and * quantifiers. Interpolated regexes use a different node type and are
# never visited. Escaped tokens (preceded by a backslash) are left alone.
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/regex_literal.rb:13
class Mutineer::Mutators::RegexLiteral < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Visits regular-expression literals.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::RegularExpressionNode] node to inspect.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/regex_literal.rb:18
  def visit_regular_expression_node(node); end

  private

  # Emits a regex mutation.
  #
  # @param start_offset [Integer] byte start.
  # @param end_offset [Integer] byte end.
  # @param replacement [String] replacement text.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/regex_literal.rb:62
  def emit(start_offset, end_offset, replacement); end

  # Scans the pattern bytes and emits anchor/quantifier mutations.
  #
  # @param content [String] regex pattern between the slashes.
  # @param base [Integer] byte offset of the pattern start in source.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/regex_literal.rb:32
  def scan(content, base); end

  # Whether the pattern ends with an unescaped +$+ anchor.
  #
  # @param content [String] regex pattern.
  # @return [Boolean] true when a trailing anchor is droppable.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/regex_literal.rb:52
  def trailing_anchor?(content); end
end

# Rescue-body mutator (Tier-2, #418).
#
# Replaces the whole body of each `rescue` clause with `nil`, and the
# value of a rescue modifier (`x rescue y` becomes `x rescue nil`). The
# mutant survives when no test checks what happens after the error.
# `statement_removal` never touches the last statement of a list, so
# without this operator a one-statement `rescue` body gets no mutant.
#
# A re-raise or a `retry` body is mutated too: `nil` swallows the error,
# which a test that expects the error kills. An empty body and a body
# that is already `nil`, `(nil)` or `()` give no mutant, because the edit
# would change nothing. An `ensure` body is not a rescue body. On the
# Fizzy copy (#418), 8 of 35 candidates give no mutant this way: 3 empty
# bodies, 3 `nil` bodies and 2 `nil` modifier values.
#
# The operator declines a body whose value is discarded and that only
# holds literals and variable reads (see docs/equivalent-skips.md).
#
# pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/rescue_body.rb:24
class Mutineer::Mutators::RescueBody < ::Mutineer::Mutators::Base
  # Collects rescue-body mutations for a subject.
  #
  # @param subject [Mutineer::Subject] subject to inspect.
  # @param source [String] full source text.
  # @return [Array<Mutineer::Mutation>] collected mutations.
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/rescue_body.rb:36
  def mutations_for(subject, source); end

  # Tracks whether this `begin` block's value, and so its rescue values, is discarded.
  #
  # @param node [Prism::BeginNode] begin block, or a def or block body with `rescue`.
  # @return [void]
  #
  # pkg:gem/mutineer#lib/mutineer/mutators/rescue_body.rb:55
  def visit_begin_node(node); end

  # Visits rescue modifiers and emits a value removal.
